    "a hand-built fixture shape hides the shape the lowering produces: a witness constructs the producer's output by hand in a shape the producer never emits, so the consumer is exercised only on the fixture and a defect on the real shape stays green. The invalid state is a consumer that answers one shape and is never run on the other.",

    "SPECIMEN, found 2026-10-20 on #14531. Swift's `T?` type position: main lowered the source `T?` (body_lower_type_wrap_occupancy) to a ONE-child Occupancy node, and the Swift type-expression projection serialized it as the malformed `X<>`. The claim swift_occupancy_emits_optional_with_every_layer_spelled_holds passed because it hand-built a TWO-child Occupancy (an `Optional` atom head plus the payload), a shape no lowering produced. Executed route and answer were both unexamined: the fixture agreed with the consumer, not with the producer.",

    "THE SECOND HALF, same PR: after the cut the lowered `T?` is an Instantiation over the std.optional Optional declaration reference, and the Swift claim was re-pinned to `Gunbc.std.optional.Optional<X>` -- the consumer's output taken as the oracle. The keyed optional_instantiation_form selected the `<...>` shape but the head was projected as an ordinary declaration reference, so Swift never spelled its own Optional (The Swift Programming Language, Language Reference > Types > Optional Type: `T?` is sugar for `Optional<T>`). The repair is a target-owned head spelling on the projection row (TargetTypeExpressionProjection optional_head: Swift Optional, Rust Option), supplied to the fold by the row and never read off the declaration path.",

    "RUNG FOUND AT: mitigatable (a green hand-built claim). RUNG NOW: mechanically preventable for the spelling (the Swift claim pins the spec spelling); NOT yet for the shape question, because swift_te_optional still builds the Instantiation by hand. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER: one claim whose Swift `T?` input is produced by the body lowering itself, which is the pairing obligation of DESIGN section 3. The head spelling stays a per-target row until std.optional carries its own per-language projection row (DESIGN section 4, one grammar read in both directions).",

"RECOGNITION RULE: a claim whose input node is built with Node { ... } literals beside a lowering that produces that node must also carry one input produced BY the lowering (the pairing obligation of DESIGN section 3), and an expected string that is the consumer's current output is not an oracle -- cite the language spec for the spelling.",

    "SPECIMEN, found 2026-10-23 (SEAT 16 N7 infer wall). #14749 (c5d5b2025e4e, a declaration's own type parameter is a known rigid type in its scope) made the fixture's distinct-occurrence discipline LOAD-BEARING. v2.test.lens_common.infer_fixture records the discipline verbatim: every authored position carries its own minted occurrence, as the real front end's do, because raw node equality compares occurrence and two synthetic atoms of one spelling are ONE key. Hand-built fixture trees that gave every in-scope type-variable position the shared synthetic spelling key stayed green while nothing derived a type for those positions; the first rule that does derive one -- the rigid-binder leaf rule, which mints v2.compiler.infer.infer_open_type_param_atom, an OccurrenceSynthetic atom -- collides the derived atom with the very node it grounds, and v2.std.constraints.canonical_grounding_from_derived_type refuses the whole tree for self-evidence (grounding_evidence_is_source) BEFORE the claimed verdict is reached. A behavior claim silently stops testing behavior: two claims of v2.test.claim.compiler.infer_application_argument_inhabitance_witness (the generic-formal refusal and the counted arrow-formal frontier) flipped to that refusal at c5d5b2025e4e while their trees still refused, for the wrong reason. Measured by claim_batch on that module at the bisect points (19 PASS / 3 FAIL before the fixture repair, 20 / 2 after the surviving reason moved with #14644, 22 / 0 expected after the mint repair), and by the module's own claims; the fixture repair mints each T position (v2.test.lens_common.infer_fixture claim_minted_atom, distinct ids) and leaves the assertions as the behavior assertions.",

    "ROOT GENERALIZATION, same specimen: the class recurs in ANY fixture that builds an in-scope type-variable position with the shared synthetic spelling key. Fork census at this head, by grep over fixtures that feed v2.compiler.infer.infer with hand-built binder scopes: two siblings carry the shape -- v2.test.claim.type_param_binder_frame (tpb_binder_edge builds each binder target, and tpb_formal each formal's declared type, from dag_type_atom_node of one name, so binder, formal and any codomain share one key) and v2.test.claim.compiler.infer_declared_return_inhabitance_witness (dr_arrow stamps the SAME return_type node into two positional codomain edges, and dr_generic_arrow's binder atom shares the key with a return type when the formal mentions the parameter). Neither module is in the executed set (the class of the sibling row gunbc.recurring_failure_mode witness_module_absent_from_the_executed_set), so their claims' status is UNMEASURED, not green: the trigger for minting them is their enrolment, and an enroller must mint first, as here.",

